# atlas-02: a direct test of AGGR's recursive cue expansion (Plan 26
# Phase 6) - the case Phase 6 exists for and atlas-01 never exercises
# (its three questions all resolve from the query's own seed entity in
# one hop). The gold answer here is reachable only by discovering a
# second entity from a first-hop memory, then traversing from it.
#
#   e1 (bridge)   - mentions BOTH atlas-project and northwind-sync. The
#                   only document CueExtractor's "atlas" seed can reach
#                   directly.
#   e2,e4,e5,e6   - decoys, mentioning atlas-project only, each sharing
#                   "Atlas"/"SSO"/"rollout" with the question. A
#                   content-only retriever (hybrid search, vector
#                   search) has every lexical reason to rank one of
#                   these in the top 3 - each is still the wrong answer.
#   e3 (gold)     - mentions northwind-sync only, never "Atlas", "SSO"
#                   or "rollout" at all. Reachable only by: seed
#                   atlas-project -> e1 -> discover northwind-sync -> e3.
#                   One hop short of this, the correct answer is
#                   unreachable by construction, and lexically it has
#                   nothing pulling it into a content-only top 3 either.
#
# A system that only does single-pass graph traversal (Phases 1-4
# without Phase 6) finds e1 and stops - northwind-sync is never tried as
# a seed, because nothing re-runs the graph build from a node discovered
# mid-traversal. A system with Phase 6 wired in re-seeds from e1's own
# entities and reaches e3 in round two. Six decoys plus the bridge and
# gold event make top_k=3 a real cut, not a trivial "return everything."
#
# 2026-11-12: all seven events share one timestamp. The original dataset
# spread them across a week (e1 Feb 1 through e7 Feb 7), which turned out
# to confound the test it was built for: CognitiveEngine's base-level
# activation (B_i = ln(n) - d*ln(t+1) + S_i) gives a newer event less
# decay penalty than an older one, and at ConversationMemory's decay rate
# that recency gap was large enough on its own to decide the ranking -
# e3's spreading-activation term (the thing this dataset exists to test)
# never got to be the deciding factor, because every event's own age was
# already deciding it first. One shared timestamp removes that confound:
# every document now has identical decay, so any ranking difference is
# attributable to spreading activation, not to which day the event
# happened to be written.

